  "sourceFindings": [
    "Prayer 446–453 adds eight complete selected numbered reports, twenty-one English segments and seven complete digital chapters (72–78). All eight Arabic paragraphs and all prior 445 entry objects remain unchanged. Complete source chains, uncertainty and transmission notes remain in the source order.",
    "Historical print PDF pages 126–128 and modern print PDF pages 123–124 were visually inspected. Modern report labels 458–465 were compared individually. The pinned English labels are 1.8.448, 1.8.449, 1.8.450f, 1.8.450m and 1.8.451–1.8.454; the two literal suffixes are preserved and each comparison has its own source/record hash.",
    "446 retains the man-or-woman uncertainty and the selected change from masculine to feminine pronouns. 448 keeps its abbreviated wording, unnamed uncertainty and closing grave-prayer clause; the latter is missing from the checked Khan-family 1.8.450f comparison. Neither report is silently harmonized with another witness or filled from its longer parallel.",
    "449 retains both opening transmitters, the embedded or words like that note, the full selected Quranic quotation including forgive me, and the final Rawh statement. The historical print names Allah in the final clause, while the selected source and modern print leave a pronoun. The English does not insert a named subject. The Bewley quotation omits forgive me; the Arabic witnesses and Khan-family comparison include it.",
    "450 retains its explicit hearing link, complete release/washing/testimony and original Arabic name spelling. 451 retains the full tent interruption and selected bleeding wording. Its final pronoun and print variants remain documented; Al-Ayni’s lexical discussion informs a provisional arm-vein rendering and records both tent and wound readings. No added historical participants or modern anatomical diagnosis enters the report.",
    "452 retains both opening recitation phrases and does not add a camel to the numbered report. 453 is a separate report with its own full chain; two original preceding newline characters remain accounted for by the existing structural map. Both prints have an untitled chapter before 453 that is absent from the digital source; this difference is disclosed without inventing a thematic heading. Names present only in the historical print and explanatory miracle language from English comparisons are not inserted.",
    "Three original Arabic introductions in chapters 74, 76 and 78 remain separately browseable and available offline. Their English translation remains pending; none enters a numbered daily session or full narration script. All seven other active book files, existing recordings and artwork remain unchanged."
  ],
